If you wanna be sure, just put your stuff in a bag, chest or a large 
box. Doesn't even need to be locked. Monsters never loot those. If 
you're leaving your gear on the floor, however, (from my experience) 
monsters will usually pick up at least weapons, armor, wands, potions, 
gold, scrolls and amulets, if they're capable of using them and don't 
have to go too far from attacking you.

What you wanna do is just leave everything next to the stairs and exit 
the level, since monsters freeze whenever you leave a level. The only 
thing that happens outside of your current dungeon level is that corpses 
rot and disappear. Everything else stays in place.

Also, monsters don't know how to unlock doors so you could as well just 
find a room with doors and lock em with a key. Locked and closed doors 
are different, almost any monster with hands (or the like) can open a 
closed door. Some oozes and the like can literally ooze under a door to 
enter a locked room, but only gelatinous cubes eat equipment. Highly 
unlikely that one would be generated and would eat your stuff while 
you're making your way downstairs. And it's not that likely that a group 
of orcs would be generated in your stash room either.

Generally, in this game you only leave stuff you don't think you'll need 
anymore so leaving stuff behind has never been a problem for me. Potions 
are needed to make holy water and scrolls to make blank scrolls, so you 
keep both. They don't weight much anyway, it's usually the armor that 
gets you burdened.

You keep amulets until identified, and throw away useless ones. Save the 
rest for eating them later or using if needed (magical breathing, ESP, 
life saving, reflection). Wands you keep until they run out of charges, 
then drop. Rather be burdened for a while, than drop stuff you have to 
come back for anyway. Spellbooks are the only exception, I leave em 
behind if burdened since only gnomish/orcish wizards pick those up.

